// ALG1 Unit 04 Lesson 05 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Interpreting Function Behavior
// MLA Standards: MLA.A1.AR.8; MLA.A1.FN.3

::ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A function starts at \(y=4\) when \(x=0\). What key feature is described?
{
~x-intercept#An x-intercept occurs when the output is 0. Here the input is 0 and the output is 4.
=y-intercept#Correct. The y-intercept is the output when the input is 0.
~zero#A zero is an input that makes the output 0. The output here is 4.
~maximum#A starting value is not automatically the highest output.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A graph crosses the x-axis at \(x=6\). What does this mean?
{
~The y-intercept is 6.#The y-intercept happens when x = 0. Crossing the x-axis means y = 0.
~The maximum output is 6.#An x-axis crossing does not identify the highest output.
=The input 6 gives an output of 0.#Correct. A zero or x-intercept occurs when the output is 0.
~The output is 6 when the input is 0.#This reverses the meaning of the x-intercept.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.8]
For \(d(x)=|x-5|\), what is the minimum output value?
{
=0#Correct. Absolute value measures distance, and the smallest possible distance is 0.
~5#The expression is centered at 5, but the output can be 0 when x = 5.
~-5#Absolute value outputs are never negative.
~10#This may occur for some inputs, but it is not the minimum.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
A height function increases from \(t=0\) to \(t=4\), then decreases from \(t=4\) to \(t=8\). What happens at \(t=4\)?
{
~The graph has a zero.#A zero happens when the output is 0. The description focuses on changing from increasing to decreasing.
=The graph reaches a maximum.#Correct. A function that changes from increasing to decreasing reaches a highest point at the change.
~The y-intercept is 4.#The y-intercept is the output when the input is 0.
~The function is constant.#A function that increases and then decreases is not constant over the whole interval.
}

::ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U04_L05_GP_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.3]
In a cost function, the y-intercept is 25. What is the best interpretation?
{
~The cost is always $25.#A y-intercept is a starting value, not necessarily every output.
~The cost increases by $25 each time.#Increase per step is rate of change, not the y-intercept.
~The function has a zero at 25.#A zero is an input where the output is 0, not a starting cost.
=The initial cost is $25 before any additional items.#Correct. The y-intercept is the output when the input is 0.
}
